WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Speaker 1: Let's go to Tim from Binghamton. Tim,&nbsp;
welcome to the program. What's on your mind today?&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.780
Speaker 3: Thank you, David. Do you&nbsp;
hear me okay? I can hear you fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.780
Awesome. So I'm a teacher. I've called him&nbsp;
before a long time listener. I think you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.760
one of the best voices on the left. Thank you&nbsp;
for everything that you do. Pleasure. And I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:27.600
want to talk about an epidemic happening in our&nbsp;
schools. But teaching for six years now and I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:35.520
watched the progression of the addiction to cell&nbsp;
phones with kids reach a remarkable level. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.860
something that, you know, we ask them as teachers,&nbsp;
we don't have a universal policy, but we ask them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.360
hey, put these phones in a bucket during the&nbsp;
instruction time and we're going to try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.800
you focused. And they cling on to them almost&nbsp;
like heroin as yeah. And I, I just don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.820
if there's enough national attention around how&nbsp;
we are bringing these kids up with these devices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.769
And I just I want to know your thoughts on how&nbsp;
we might approach this as a national discussion.&nbsp;

00:01:06.769 --> 00:01:12.060
Speaker 1: I'm basically with you as to the&nbsp;
level of problem that this is. I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.740
really is an epidemic. I think there's a&nbsp;
couple of different things here. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:22.320
separate issue from adult so-called addiction to&nbsp;
social media. Specifically, I think there's two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.400
different things and we want to make sure we deal&nbsp;
with them differently. What you're talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.420
I see it firsthand and a lot of my friends who&nbsp;
have kids and from those who teach in schools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:40.680
I also hear it and there clearly is something&nbsp;
physical that goes on with these screens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.460
even like with my baby daughter who's not even six&nbsp;
months old sometimes. Like if she's just handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:53.520
to me for four, 2 minutes and I'm sitting at my&nbsp;
desk, even if I face her completely away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.960
screens, she starts craning her neck, trying to&nbsp;
get a look at the screens. And we don't show her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:04.080
any screens. Right? So I just basically walk out&nbsp;
of the room. It does seem that there is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:13.200
just physical going on here. And my concern is&nbsp;
attention spans, inability to focus, inability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.860
to do what Cal Newport calls deep work. So at the&nbsp;
societal level or at schools, I have no idea what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.080
the solution should be. I know I'm going to take&nbsp;
a personal approach, you know, and for now it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:31.200
basically no screens, period. And we'll revisit it&nbsp;
at some future period, you know, future point. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.620
no, I agree with you. It's an absolute epidemic.
Speaker 3: Yeah. One last quick point. I did work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.900
at a charter school for a brief amount of time&nbsp;
where they took the phones out the door. Yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:48.600
And that had a huge impact on teaching and&nbsp;
learning. It was incredibly powerful. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:55.860
don't know if public schools have the resources&nbsp;
they need to. I mean, it'll take an army to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.480
the phones at the door and it's going to be a&nbsp;
battle. And it's and it's something that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.729
parents aren't all on board with because they tax&nbsp;
their kids in school. It's an absolute nightmare.&nbsp;

00:03:04.729 --> 00:03:08.940
Speaker 1: That's what I do, which is I know&nbsp;
people who teach at charter schools who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.780
had good luck with that. They even have&nbsp;
these devices that like you hold the phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.740
but the phone is in a device. I forget what the&nbsp;
name of it is. You just can't use it until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:23.100
end of the day. But there is that issue where&nbsp;
for for many kids, parents are relying on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.400
in case they need to communicate anything&nbsp;
in cases of emergency or whatever. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.920
know how frequent that is. I don't know how&nbsp;
good of an argument that is. But I think even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.260
parents would respond times. It's not censorship.
Speaker 3: It's nonsense. It is. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.780
anyway, I'll let you get some more calls. You're&nbsp;
awesome, David. Keep up the great work. Happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.920
holidays and enjoy the time with your family.
Speaker 1: All right. Thank you. And likewise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.440
Big, big time holiday coming&nbsp;
up here in the United States.

